WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.305
At one point, BlackBerry was one of the most powerful

2
00:00:03.385 --> 00:00:07.336
names in smartphones. So how did it lose its place in the market?

3
00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:11.283
BlackBerry built its reputation on secure messaging,

4
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.015
physical keyboards, and business communication.

5
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.702
By 2009, it held almost 20 percent of the global smartphone market.

6
00:00:19.421 --> 00:00:20.843
Then the market changed.

7
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.149
Apple's iPhone introduced a new kind of smartphone experience,

8
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.120
while Android brought touchscreen devices to a growing range

9
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:34.040
of manufacturers. BlackBerry struggled to respond quickly enough.

10
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.469
It underestimated how much consumers valued large touchscreens,

11
00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.941
broader app ecosystems, and a different mobile experience.

12
00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.498
The consequences were enormous.

13
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:48.850
BlackBerry's global smartphone unit market share fell from around 20

14
00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:53.066
percent in 2009 to almost nothing by 2016.

15
00:00:56.433 --> 00:01:00.050
BlackBerry eventually stopped developing its own phone hardware and shifted

16
00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.787
its focus toward software, cybersecurity,

17
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.124
and secure communications. The company didn't disappear.

18
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:06.985
It changed what it

19
00:01:07.065 --> 00:01:11.887
was. Here's the lesson: your biggest advantage today can become

20
00:01:11.967 --> 00:01:13.450
your biggest weakness tomorrow.

21
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.415
Success requires more than protecting what works.

22
00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:19.556
It requires adapting before the market leaves you behind.
